athcal{C})$", "MATH_0003": "$U \\in \\Ob(\\mathcal{C})$", "MATH_0004": "$$\n\\textit{PSh}(\\mathcal{C})\n\\longrightarrow\n\\textit{Sets}, \\quad\n\\mathcal{F}\n\\longmapsto\n\\mathcal{F}(U)\n$$", "MATH_0005": "$\n\\mathcal{F} :\n\\mathcal{I}\n\\to\n\\textit{PSh}(\\mathcal{C})\n$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F}_{\\lim} \\to \\mathcal{F}_i$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F}_i \\to \\mathcal{F}_{\\colim}$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0009": "$(\\mathcal{G}, q_i : \\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{F}_i)$", "MATH_0010": "$U$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{G}(U) \\to \\mathcal{F}_i(U)$", "MATH_0012": "$\\mathcal{G}(U) \\to \\mathcal{F}_{\\lim}(U)$", "MATH_0013": "$U$", "MATH_0014": "$\\mathcal{G} \\to \\mathcal{F}_{\\lim}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-definition-limit}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{categories-definition-colimit}", "SPACE_0001": "\\ ", "SPACE_0002": "\\ ", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 2, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:a1f32d27d80e411528b5b2b97d00d7c57a28d88c944811c3142f0dfec681e127", "source_math_skeleton_hash": "sha256:74f4e9f0d0a33181a4f4c558ed50a58829a0ecc5fb8a2d45a37171e6d8b8dceb", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:99de5006237be547e69caa14b29e064cf861cfa1c9535b2842b33a19c60b6c78", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nLet <MATH_0001> be a category.\nLimits and colimits exist in the category\n<MATH_0002>. In addition, for any\n<MATH_0003> the functor\n<MATH_0004>\ncommutes with limits and colimits. Perhaps the easiest way to prove\nthese statements is the following. Given a diagram\n<MATH_0005>\ndefine presheaves\n<MATHSEG_0001>  and  <MATHSEG_0002>\nThere are clearly projection maps <MATH_0006>\nand canonical maps <MATH_0007>. These\nmaps satisfy the requirements of the maps of a limit (resp.<SPACE_0001>colimit)\nof Categories, Definition <REF_0001>\n(resp.<SPACE_0002>Categories, Definition <REF_0002>).\nIndeed, they clearly form a cone, resp. a cocone, over <MATH_0008>.\nFurthermore, if <MATH_0009>\nis another\nsystem (as in the definition of a limit), then we get for every\n<MATH_0010> a system of maps <MATH_0011>\nwith suitable functoriality requirements. And thus a unique\nmap <MATH_0012>. It is easy\nto verify these are compatible  as we vary <MATH_0013> and arise from\nthe desired map <MATH_0014>.\nA similar argument works in the case of the colimit.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:d21e4eaa65c8fa8f894c12562fd31c8574e296230a598f5914ff0c8213ca46ac", "unit_id": "tag:00VB:prose-0001"}
